@fields
{
Traditions :: Divine, Occult
}


@handout
{
You dreamt of a riverbank. Wildflowers, unfamiliar yet beautiful, cradled your body. The warmth of [[Godhome|twin suns]] kissed your face, and a soft breeze carried the scent of earth and water. Across the river, a grand city rose—its architecture strange, unplaceable.

Someone lay beside you in the flowers. They were breathtaking, yet their features slipped from your mind before they could settle, like mist in the morning light. They sang.

The song held you, bound by its melody. Minutes, hours—it didn’t matter. The tune was haunting, filled with loneliness, longing. Even as the dream faded, pulling you back to waking, the song remained.

It had carved itself into your soul.

Now awake, it lingers at the edge of perception. You cannot recall the notes, yet the melody is unmistakable—a funeral dirge, or a call to action.

Months pass. Others have heard it too. They surface in radio broadcasts, in screamsheet editorials, in whispered conversations. Some call it revelation. Others claim it’s a trap. Some fear it is not of this world at all.

They call it the Voidsong.

The world is divided. The faithful and the fearful, the seekers and the skeptics. You know only one thing: it is real.

And it wants something.

Whatever life you led before, the Voidsong has marked you. The need for answers burns within you. Your search leads you to [[Geography/Calaria/Hallia/index|Hallia]], to [[Org/Iconoclasm/index|Iconoclasm]].
}

The song lives only in the heads of those who hear it; there is no song out in the world to touch. A [[Phenomena/Sentience Distributor|sentience distributor]] is built around carrying a harmony, but it cannot carry the Voidsong without some missing piece of hardware or a magical connection that would turn the song in a listener's head into a signal the device could spread.

Neither the Voidsong nor the harmonies of the [[Org/Embercall Industries/index|Embercall]] can be written out as sheet music. [[Org/Roundhat Gang/People/Tywelwyn Leatherhide|Tywelwyn]] mapped the Embercall by writing a set of pieces that were each a constituent part of what he heard, in the way an abstract painting can stand in for a thing that cannot be looked at directly. [[People/Anzu|Anzu]] did the same with the Voidsong and produced a sheet of music that hangs a little out of the world: translucent, strangely heavy, and pulling away. Gathered into one object, the song might pull something toward the void. The void paper resonates faintly on the same frequency as the song. The statue at the shrine in the [[Geography/Calaria/Hallia/Sewers|royal waterways]] resonates as loudly as a person does, on a different one.

Many have heard the song. When [[People/Johnny|Johnny]] resonated in the crowd at the [[Geography/Calaria/Ragged Roost|Ragged Roost]] pilgrimage, some forty of the guests answered.

A Belvedere archival unit held a new artifact touched by the Voidsong until someone broke into the facility loudly, passed its defences, and carried it off. It was a green rock, twelve-sided, floating in a golden case, carrying a harmonic resonance like the song. Anzu reads keystone 14 as a sealed compartment that only receives, and Belvedere believes the two are related.

The [[Org/Embercall Industries/index|Embercall]] is built in the song's likeness but not its equal: where the Voidsong passes through the [[Phenomena/Firmament|firmament]] untouched, the Embercall eats into it, and it must be held into the world by a living, thinking antenna. The Embercall's researchers concluded that the Voidsong could be reworked only with the cooperation or coercion of many of its listeners, which is why they want the [[Phenomena/Pilgrimage|pilgrimage]] and the source of the song.

The [[Org/Protectorate/index|Protectorate]] hears the song as the voice of Aurelia I, who its people believe is trapped on the horizon and singing for rescue. Its member [[Org/Protectorate/People/Yao Addington|Yao Addington]] counts it a call for help with intention in it, and many listeners from [[Geography/Brithwyn/index|Brithwyn]] read it the same way.

The song is not always the same volume in a listener's head. [[People/Anzu|Anzu]] heard it faint once before, when he looked on the [[Divinity/Ashen Pact/Giant|Giant]], and fainter than that when the [[Org/The Harlequins/Providence|Providence]] came to rest off the charted horizon far starward.

The song can be passed from one listener to another. [[People/Anzu|Anzu]] has done it once, putting the Voidsong into [[People/Egan Arithmites|Egan]]'s head in place of the [[Divinity/Ashen Pact/Herald|Herald]]'s song.
